Q: `reinterpret_cast` between unrelated structs A: <p>The Standard specifies that a <code>reinterpret_cast</code> between object pointers is the equivalent of a double <code>static_cast</code> from <code>A*</code> to <code>void*</code> and then to <code>B*</code>. ([expr.reinterpret.cast]/7). When the types are unrelated but have the same object representations, this will work on compilers in 2026, but there could be a standard-conforming implementation where it does not.  For example, the runtime is allowed to keep track of which objects it created where, and crash if the program attempts to access a memory address through an incompatible pointer. It’s even plausible to me that an implementation designed for memory safety would.</p>
<p>One standard way to do this is to make each <code>struct</code> (which must be a C-like “standard-layout <code>struct</code>,” not something complex like an object with <code>virtual</code> functions or inheritance) a member of a <code>union</code>.  If the first one or more members of the structures are layout-compatible, you can then type-pun on those members through a different <code>struct</code> in the <code>union</code>.  To quote the Standard ([class.union.general], note 1):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One special guarantee is made in order to simplify the use of unions: If a standard-layout union contains several standard-layout structs that share a common initial sequence, and if a non-static data member of an object of this standard-layout union type is active and is one of the standard-layout structs, it is permitted to inspect the common initial sequence of any of the standard-layout struct members</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So for example,</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>    union {
        A a = {.x = 1, .y = 2.0f, .z = 3.0, .arr = {}};
        B b;
    }; // Type-pun from A to B through an anonymous union.

    std::cout &lt;&lt; b.x &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.y &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.z &lt;&lt; '\n';
</code></pre>
<p>If the structures can be type-punned by copying their object representations with <code>memcpy()</code>, you can also convert from one to the other using <code>std::bit_cast</code>.  If the source object is correctly-aligned for the type you want to convert to, <code>std::start_lifetime_as</code> is how you replace an object with a new object of a different type, at the same memory location, with the same object representation. (This <strong>does not</strong> let you break the Strict-Aliasing Rule: the original object that was there is replaced.) See [obj.lifetime].</p>

Q: How to paginate HTML content for printing while keeping header and footer on every page A: <p>Since when using <code>window.print()</code>, you need:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Header and footer repeated on every printed page</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Each section (Lab, Radiology, etc.) starts on a new page</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Long sections that overflow still get the header/footer on continuation pages</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>You may just use tables instead of div sections to set your layout, since when printed their header and footer get repeated:</p>
<p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/tbody#usage_notes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/tbody#usage_notes</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>When printing a document, in the case of a multipage table, the &lt;thead&gt; and &lt;tfoot&gt; elements usually specify information that remains the same—or at least very similar—on each page, while the &lt;tbody&gt; element's contents generally will differ from page to page.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here I simplified your scenario just to showcase the core problem and demonstrate how those requirements get fulfilled:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@media print {

  .no-print {
    display: none;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .new-section {
    page-break-before: always;
  }

  tfoot td {
    position: fixed;
    bottom: 10mm;
    width: 100%;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;body&gt;

  &lt;button class="no-print" onclick="window.print()"&gt;Print&lt;/button&gt;

  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;thead&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;
          &lt;h2&gt;My Header&lt;/h2&gt;
          &lt;hr&gt;
        &lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;/thead&gt;

    &lt;tfoot&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;
          &lt;hr&gt;&lt;small&gt;My Footer&lt;/small&gt;
        &lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;/tfoot&gt;

    &lt;tbody&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;Section 1 content — can be as long as needed,
          header/footer repeat automatically on overflow.&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr class="new-section"&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;Section 2 — forced to a new page.&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr class="new-section"&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;Section 3 — also a new page.&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;/tbody&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
